gh  scheme that huge  government intervention that we saw but  just look at these bits  look at them on the record the last year  or you don't i'm not suggesting we  ignore that of course they've taken  measures um  we've supported those measures we think  they should have gone further in various  places but that's to one side  but they've done that for necessity the  idea that this is some conversion from  the  uh conservative party a way that you can  conveniently forget the last 10 years  they've  converted to social democracy i think is  completely  wrong no i'm keen to find out more about  you about what you believe in and the  kind of country that you want to build  so early this month the labour  strategy document was linked to the  guardian and they talked to focus groups  who  said that people are confused about what  we stand for and what our purpose is  and they also think that you need to  stop sitting on the fence so i want to  find out  a bit more about the real care stamina  you know banning the kind of  generalities that no one would disagree  with like fairness  what is it that you really stand for a  driving determination  to root out inequality and insecurity in  our country and our society in an  economy  and to build a better future for britain  yes i'm patriotic that was one of the  issues that was in this  report that was lee of course i'm  patriotic  i want to be prime minister of this  country because i want this country to  be even better than it is  now actually the whole labour movement  is very patriotic we  we are in politics to change our country  for the better  you can't be more patriotic than that  and i'm very very comfortable with it  i just wonder if it does sound quite  general still though doesn't it that you  want to build a better country that you  want to stop inequality i mean no one  would really disagree with that  would they well i don't think it's  general to say that  the economic model that we've had for a  decade  has completely failed and we need to  sweep it away and bring in something  where you have an active  government tackling inequality tackling  insecurity  and working actively with business as a  business partnership  going forward and the the  forward-looking businesses  know that the days of deregulation the  days when you don't need to worry about  the climate  uh emergency are over and actually  there's a real opportunity there  to to shape a better britain for the  next generation  that is what pulses through my veins  that's what i want  to achieve and if we can take the labor  party from where it is now to where we  need to be to win the next general  election  that is what we'll do okay i'm keen to  talk about a few specifics now just to  get a bit of a sense about where you  start stand on some of these key issues  so immigration in the labour leadership  you promise to defend free movement as  we leave the eu you've now dropped that  pledge  well we've left the eu and free um  movement is gone  is the reality of the situation and  we'll dave we'll face a different  situation in 2024 because we have left  free movement has gone would you like to  have free movement well we're going to  have to have a fair and humane  immigration system and we will set out  what that is in 2024 but  uh free movement is gone and and you  can't flick a switch  uh on this free movement depended on  membership of the  eu depending on membership of the single  market and we're at a different place  now how about drugs do you think there's  a case for  decriminalizing cannabis possession i've  never subscribed to that  view when i was director of public  prosecutions  i prosecuted many many cases or my team  did  involving drugs and drug gangs and the  criminality that sits behind  and it causes huge um issues  to vulnerable people across the country  so i've never gone down that  route there were some initiatives in  some parts of the country where  cautions were given for low-leve